                'quiz_data' => [
                    'question' => 'If a PSX stock is trading at PKR 120 per share and has an annual EPS of PKR 15, what is its P/E ratio?',
                    'options' => ['6.0x', '8.0x', '12.0x', '15.0x'],
                    'correct_index' => 1,
                    'explanation' => 'P/E = Market Price (PKR 120) / Earnings Per Share (PKR 15) = 8.0x. This means you are paying 8 times annual earnings.',
                ],
                'urdu_body' => "P/E Ratio (Price-to-Earnings) ka aasaan matlab yeh hai ke kisi company ke Rs. 1 saalana profit ke peechay aap stock market mein kitnay Rupay ada kar rahay hain.\n\nMisal ke tor par: Agar Fauji Fertilizer ka share price Rs. 120 hai aur saalana per-share profit (EPS) Rs. 15 hai, to P/E ratio hoga 120 / 15 = 8x. Is ka matlab aap 8 saal ke profit ke barabar qeemat de rahay hain.\n\nPSX par aam tor par P/E ratios dunya ke muqablay mein kafi saste hotay hain (4x se 7x). Lekin hamesha ek hi sector ki companies ka aapas mein muqabla karein, misal ke tor par MCB ka muqabla HBL se karein, cement company se nahi.",
                'body' => "The Price-to-Earnings (P/E) ratio is the single most widely referenced valuation metric for evaluating companies listed on the Pakistan Stock Exchange (PSX).\n\n### What Does P/E Tell You?\nAt its core, the P/E ratio tells you how many Rupees investors are willing to pay for every Rs. 1 of annual earnings generated by the business. It is calculated as:\n\nP/E Ratio = Current Market Price per Share / Earnings Per Share (EPS)\n\nFor example, if a blue-chip company like Engro Corporation is trading at PKR 300 per share and its audited trailing twelve-month earnings per share (EPS) is PKR 50, its P/E ratio is 300 / 50 = 6.0x.\n\n### Evaluating PSX P/E Multiples\nGlobally, emerging and frontier markets trade at P/E multiples between 12x and 20x. In contrast, the Pakistan Stock Exchange has historically traded at deep value multiples ranging between 4x and 8x.\n\nWhen evaluating a stock:\n1. **Peer Comparison**: Compare cement companies (Lucky Cement vs Pioneer Cement) within their own sector. Never compare a capital-heavy cement firm with a high-growth technology stock like Systems Limited.\n2. **Historical Mean**: Check if the stock is trading below or above its 5-year historical average multiple.\n3. **Quality of Earnings**: Ensure profits are backed by actual operating cash flows rather than one-time asset sales.",
            ],
